{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 15 &middot; partial transforms</p>
    <h1>Prices</h1>
    <p>Everything we pour at the bar, and every coffee we sell by the bag.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="stack">
    <section class="card">
      {{> "prices/bar" caption="At the bar"}}
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      {{> "prices/beans" caption="Beans to take home"}}
    </section>
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <strong>Both tables are spreadsheets.</strong> <code>src/partials/prices/bar.csv</code>
    and <code>beans.csv</code> are plain CSV, the kind any spreadsheet exports, so whoever
    sets the prices edits a spreadsheet and never touches the markup. The page calls each one
    like any partial, with a caption as an argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The site taught kiss the <code>.csv</code> extension.</strong> In
    <code>router.js</code>, <code>transforms: &#123; csv &#125;</code> says what to do with one,
    and <code>transforms.js</code> holds it. Its <code>render</code> gets the file and the
    call's arguments and returns the table, escaping every cell. It sets
    <code>compile: false</code>, because a price list is data and not a template: the file
    reaches <code>render</code> exactly as written.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
